= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $L=\mathbb Q(\alpha)$, where $\alpha$ is an <algebraic integer> with monic <minimal polynomial> $f\in\mathbb Z[X]$, and put $I=[\mathcal O_L:\mathbb Z[\alpha]]$. The <Kummer-Dedekind theorem> applies to a <prime number> $p$ with $p\nmid I$. Write its reduction as a product of <polynomial factors> over $\mathbb F_p$:
$$
\overline f=\prod_{j=1}^r g_j^{e_j},
$$
where the $g_j$ are distinct monic <irreducible polynomials>, choose monic integral lifts $G_j$. Then the distinct <prime ideals> above $p$ are $\mathfrak p_j=(p,G_j(\alpha))$, and
$$
\boxed{p\mathcal O_L=\prod_{j=1}^r\mathfrak p_j^{e_j},\qquad f(\mathfrak p_j/p)=\deg g_j.}
$$
Thus the multiplicity of a factor is its <ramification index of a prime ideal>, and its degree is the <residue degree>. The hypothesis on the <order in a number field> is essential: one cannot infer <prime ideal factorization> from an arbitrary defining polynomial at a prime dividing its index. There is also a relative version over a base <number field> $K$: if $\mathcal O_L$ and $\mathcal O_K[\alpha]$ agree after localization at $\mathfrak p$, factor the <minimal polynomial> over $\mathcal O_K/\mathfrak p$ and use $(\mathfrak p,G_j(\alpha))$ with the same conclusions.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <polynomial discriminant> satisfies
$$
\operatorname{disc}(f)=(-1)^{n(n-1)/2}\operatorname{Res}(f,f'),\qquad n=\deg f.
$$
A polynomial has no repeated roots over the algebraic closure exactly when it is coprime to its derivative. Consequently the assumed squarefree reduction has nonzero <resultant> with its derivative, so $p\nmid\operatorname{disc}(f)$. On the other hand the power basis is a basis of the <order in a number field> $\mathbb Z[\alpha]$, and the <discriminant-index formula for an integral lattice> gives
$$
\operatorname{disc}(f)=\operatorname{disc}(1,\alpha,\ldots,\alpha^{n-1})=[\mathcal O_L:\mathbb Z[\alpha]]^2d_L.
$$
Here the <field discriminant> $d_L$ is an integer. Therefore
$$
\boxed{p\nmid[\mathcal O_L:\mathbb Z[\alpha]].}
$$
This is <squarefree reduction excludes a prime from the power-basis index>; its argument establishes the index condition before applying the <Kummer-Dedekind theorem>.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Put $\alpha=44^{1/11}$ and $L=\mathbb Q(\alpha)$. The <Eisenstein criterion> at $11$ shows that $X^{11}-44$ is an <irreducible polynomial>, so $[L:\mathbb Q]=11$. Its derivative is $11X^{10}$, and the <polynomial discriminant> is
$$
\operatorname{disc}(X^{11}-44)=-11^{11}44^{10}=-2^{20}11^{21}.
$$
The <discriminant-index formula for an integral lattice> implies that the <field discriminant> has no prime divisor outside $\{2,11\}$. By the <discriminant obstruction to ramification>, every other prime is unramified.

To prove that neither candidate disappears through an index correction, let $\mathfrak p$ lie above $p\in\{2,11\}$ and normalize its <valuation> to have value group $\mathbb Z$. If $e=e_{\mathfrak p/p}$, the relation $\alpha^{11}=44$ gives
$$
11v_{\mathfrak p}(\alpha)=e\,v_p(44)=\begin{cases}2e,&p=2,\\e,&p=11.\end{cases}
$$
In both cases $11\mid e$. The <fundamental identity for prime decomposition>, $\sum_{\mathfrak p\mid p}e_{\mathfrak p/p}f_{\mathfrak p/p}=11$, now forces exactly one <prime ideal> above each candidate, with $e=11$ and $f=1$. This is <ramification forced by a coprime root valuation>; at $2$ it avoids an invalid direct application of the <Eisenstein criterion>, since $4\mid44$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\text{The ramified rational primes are exactly }2\text{ and }11;\ \text{both are totally ramified}.}
$$

